I think that the fourth one may be my favorite just because it makes crowd control so much easier.

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:58.220
Right. Yeah. The only thing that the fifth one gives you over the fourth one is that you can now get two extra hits or three extra hits or something before you lose it.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.880
And you're within two direct hits.

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:13.620
Okay, yeah. But also the flame shot one that Sean likes, that one makes an interesting switch off too in that, yeah, it is better than it shoots in the three directions, which is very effective for taking care of a lot of different things.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:20.700
But its damage only counts as two times normal shots, whereas penetration counted for three times.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:22.340
So you're actually by... I keep saying penetration.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:23.460
I know. It's so strange.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.860
But I don't know what else to call it.

00:13:25.960 --> 00:13:27.020
Power up three.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:42.780
You know, it's interesting that they decided to actually think about, well, if we just kept scaling it up to be like, well, this one's three times powerful, this one's four times powerful, this one's five times powerful, then you would never, you know, you would just continue to get more op throughout the game.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:50.660
And so it was cool that they thought about damage scaling as well, even if they do on the Firebird, just say, it's four times as powerful as many times.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:54.880
Like, go to town. You collected four items consecutively.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.040
You might as well...

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:56.840
You're a good boy.

00:13:57.040 --> 00:13:58.160
Yeah. Might as well keep going.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:00.860
You like collecting so much, why don't you marry it?

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:04.880
So these are the lazy jokes that we make when we're not prepared.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:07.120
Which is always, really.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:17.620
Yeah, true. So the vertical stages are nothing special, I think, in the world of shmups, because it's mostly just patterns of enemies coming down.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:20.660
It reminded me very much of Stinger with the way the enemy...

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:26.460
Or even, like, to go really far back, like Galaga, you know, where you see the enemies on screen first.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.960
They would do their, like, here we come, and then their performed layout.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:44.520
I'd like to say that I think that the concept of having ground forces as well as airborne forces and having to switch between, like, a bomb and an actual shot...

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:53.520
I kind of hate this idea in the shmups, because your bombs end up being so boring and slow.

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:56.740
And it just breaks the flow so much for me.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:01.120
Also, does it matter that much in a game where you can do two inputs at once?

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:04.740
So you're just constantly hitting both all the time?

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:07.460
Sure. No, I'm saying, like, it's stupid.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:08.820
They should actually make it this way.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:10.400
Is it one or the other?

00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:11.520
And does it really matter?

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:15.020
And also, why are those things on the ground able to hurt us?

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:17.340
We have wings.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:23.760
Yeah, we have wings. From so far away, you know, it's like, why are they a threat on that?

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:26.280
And why should you have to manage that kind of thing?

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.380
Like, there's already enough enemies up at the top.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:34.040
But I didn't enjoy the bomb not having any power-ups either.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:40.740
That was important because precision becomes a thing, specifically with the land enemies.

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:44.900
So you have to think, you know, you have to almost think about, like, lining up your shots.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.900
Yeah. And I think that the land enemies in this game specifically are a lot more trigger-happy than we've seen before.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.900
Like, I ended up having to dodge them more than I had to dodge the other enemies in the sky.

00:15:55.900 --> 00:16:03.940
Yeah, because you can't outright ignore them because if you do, they'll just continue to shoot wherever you may be on the screen, limiting your arc.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:13.880
So if there's three of them on the screen and you go from left to right, you know, sooner or later, you're not going to be able to go back to your left and you're going to trap yourself in a corner.

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.760
Whether that's good game design or not, I'll let game designers decide.

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:20.120
But I didn't enjoy that as a player.

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:26.800
Yeah, and one thing I think we didn't talk about much yet either is on, I mean, in both versions of the level, that you can't shoot behind you.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.820
Which I think things get behind me all the time in this game.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:37.200
So that adds another level of frustration, especially I noticed in the vertical levels where things would be behind me.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:41.500
You can't, I mean, I guess you can back up, but sometimes you can't back up enough.

00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:48.620
Sometimes they're at the back of the screen. I don't mind that you couldn't shoot behind you because that seems to be the standard in shmups for the most part.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:52.220
But I would have liked a power-up that maybe shot in the directions.

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:59.460
Yeah, exactly. I guess it was just that I noticed it more because it just seemed like there were so many more things behind me in this game than there are in a lot of shmups.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:00.680
I didn't notice that.

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:07.440
I think that if they made it a thing that, like, you can just shoot behind you if you do this button combination, that would just mess with me.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:11.940
So it would have to be always shooting behind you, and that would just look goofy.

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:17.800
I don't know. And I think the patterns actually didn't lend themselves to having enemies behind you.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:24.380
There were definitely enemies that would appear from behind, and you would have to, you know, first let them kind of circle back.

00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:32.980
But I think that's part of the, you know, there's this element of shmups that I'm sure we've all understood,

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:39.160
but we haven't really talked about on the podcast, of that just as much as this is about, like, shooting everything up,

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:42.920
it's also this complicated, like, self-evolving maze.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.820
Yeah. The opposite thing where you don't touch the walls.

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:49.040
The opposite thing. You know, like, rather than going along.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:52.260
It's about evasion as much as it is about dealing out damage.

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:58.820
Right, yeah. Yeah, so you really have to, it's almost like a puzzle of figuring out everybody's arc and where they're going to be,

00:17:58.820 --> 00:18:05.040
and either knowing that to shoot where they're going to wind up, or dodge to a place where they're not going to be.

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:06.320
Yeah. No, I hear you.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:10.260
And I think I'm mixing up where I have found it the most problematic.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:16.820
I think it was actually the horizontal levels, where something about seeing it horizontally makes me feel like I should be able to shoot in the other direction,

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:17.960
because it's not, like, behind.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:23.840
Well, in the horizontal levels, I felt overall, because of the different layout of the sprites,

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:29.980
and the way that the graphics look in the horizontal levels, everything felt very claustrophobic in those levels.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:37.320
There's really, you know, if there was, let's say, four lanes in the vertical one,

00:18:37.460 --> 00:18:40.900
there's only really three in a horizontal level.

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:46.060
I guess I just mean by, like, lanes of player, positions the player can be in that are completely unique.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:53.260
Yeah. So I didn't appreciate the horizontal levels at all, because not only were they claustrophobic,

00:18:53.460 --> 00:19:00.920
but they had this weird enemy that would shoot when it was directly, when you wound up directly under it.

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:08.280
And that became a game of dodging on a platformer level for a scrolling shmup,

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.560
something that you don't really have control over.

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:17.260
The enemy is, between that one that was, like, the ceiling-mounted laser and another,

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:23.620
which was sort of like a weird bug that would jump between the ceiling and the floor and shoot to the side.

00:19:23.620 --> 00:19:25.640
Was that the jellyfish thing?

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:27.000
Yeah, it kind of looked like a jellyfish.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:31.860
Okay. That made a lot of sections very difficult to figure.

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:38.100
You know what? I've just realized that in this podcast, we just want a game that serves everything to us on a silver platter.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.060
Like, anytime something's kind of hard where it's like, you know, screw this game.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:43.940
Tell us like it is, John.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:46.080
I just didn't like it.

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:47.440
I didn't like that part.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:54.200
Yeah, I don't know if I need games to be easy, to enjoy them.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:58.860
You know, it's easy to look back at all this stuff later, retrospectively,

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:05.120
and say this was dumb and was just used as a way to, you know, enhance the game's lifespan.

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:09.060
Yeah. To be like, well, you know, you just got to remember everything about the game,

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.880
and you'll get through it no problem.

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:16.120
You know, that's half of the sadistic nature of people who like shmups,

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:20.380
is that they just constantly play through the same levels over and over again

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.620
until they've figured out all the patterns and things.

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:25.580
And I find that to be very rewarding in this genre.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:33.420
I just don't really care for enemies that are either so different than everything else in the stage

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:38.240
that they become their own boss in a way.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:43.280
You know, like, every time, anytime an enemy comes up that's not a boss that you dread,

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:46.400
that's going to continue to show up throughout a game too,

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:51.160
that is more of a flaw in the design of the game for me than it is a,

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:54.740
like, if the death beams were a single level layout thing,

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:58.960
where, you know, it was just like, oh, this is that one part of the stage where that happens,

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.520
and you got to like, you know, think about your position.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:07.920
Because there are some that are, like, very well laid out where you can just choose the correct path ahead of time

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.060
and you won't have to deal with them.

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:12.260
And then there's others where it's just like, nah, we didn't think it that hard.

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:15.560
Like, they're just here all the time now, and you just got to, you know,

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:20.380
constantly be dodging them as you wait for this scrolling exit to appear.

00:21:20.380 --> 00:21:31.040
Yeah. And I think that this sort of leads into, we were talking about what pads out a game.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.960
This game is just a repeating sequence of two levels.

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:39.900
And not only that, but a repeating sequence of two bosses.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:48.360
And I felt kind of, even though I, you know, this game was released years and years ago,

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:50.880
I felt sort of robbed because of that.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:57.700
Like, it says that, like, oh, you've killed the first boss,

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:00.800
now you have four other towers to go to or something along those lines.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:02.740
But they're all the same tower.

00:22:02.900 --> 00:22:05.220
It's the same dragon with a different coat of paint.

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:12.300
It's the same weird brain mothership with more eyes.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:16.840
And we've seen plenty of games older than this that have done it better,

00:22:16.940 --> 00:22:18.020
that have given us new stuff.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:22.460
But it's even, I know that the specific patterns change,

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:28.760
and maybe the arrangement of caverns change a little bit,

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:31.060
but there isn't really too much that changes,

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:35.760
except unless you go into, like, these bonus levels where you go into the dark stomach

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:36.940
or something like that.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.540
Like, I just felt like there wasn't enough variety in this game,

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:44.120
even though some of the things felt good.

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:49.040
Yeah, I think the stuff, you know, the levels themselves feel really,

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:51.840
not talking on a gameplay level,

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.060
they feel really nicely designed aesthetically.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:57.860
And it feels like they put a lot of work into, like you said, these two levels.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:01.800
Yeah. And then it almost feels like, as much as I love the design,

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:05.680
like, even if they sacrificed a little bit of, like, the aesthetic quality of it

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:07.520
to make more variety.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:13.100
Yeah. So I can, you know. But, yeah, that is, like, I think that's, like, a big highlight of this game

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:15.160
is that, like, it looks unique and cool

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.480
until you start looping back on yourself over and over again.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:19.660
You see the same unique and cool thing.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.260
Yeah, yeah. Now it's not unique anymore.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:22.740
Now it's everywhere.

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:28.220
And there's two sub-stages specifically found in the vertical shmup levels,

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:30.740
and that's the minus stage and the bonus stage.

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:32.060
Did anybody get to either of these?

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:34.400
They're, like, those whirlpools show up.

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:35.380
One has a face.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:37.820
I got to the one in the face, the one that...

00:23:37.820 --> 00:23:39.380
The minus stage. Yes, the bad one.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:47.640
And it's just kind of another side-scrolling, very, like, organic, like, environment

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:50.560
where it feels like you're, like, inside something's brain or something.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:53.060
Yeah, you're, like, inside the brain or womb.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:55.240
Right. It has, like...

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:56.540
No, it has, like, these weird...

00:23:56.540 --> 00:23:57.660
It's more of a stomach. I think it's a stomach.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:58.560
Is it a stomach? Yeah.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:23:59.920
Well, I guess you went into the thing's mouth.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:01.460
It looks internal. Yes.

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:05.000
And it's actually pretty graphic for an NES game at the time.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:06.600
I'm surprised Nintendo was cool with that.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:08.900
Yeah. They have the final say on it.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:12.800
Like, they've made other developers change things in games that were fine in the arcade

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:13.800
versions. For sure.

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:19.700
And I actually went into that, read the first one that was available by accident, and I

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:21.560
just thought, oh, that's cool. This is what the game is going to be.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:27.500
You fly on this overworld, which is the vertical levels, and then, like, land in these horizontal

00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:30.500
levels, finish them, and then come back out and continue on the overworld, which isn't

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:32.800
what it was. See, I didn't think you were supposed to go...

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:35.420
I thought that the thing, like, sort of sucks you into it.

00:24:35.480 --> 00:24:36.860
I thought that was fire.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:38.640
Like, I thought you were supposed to avoid that.

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:40.100
Well, I think you are.

00:24:40.520 --> 00:24:45.420
But, like, the ones that I think you find just scattered around if you blow up a couple

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:48.540
of the ground-based enemies.

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:49.740
Yeah. The bonus ones.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:52.900
I thought that those were all so bad, and then I just didn't know...

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:55.140
They don't look any different other than face and no face.

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:56.960
Yeah. So, I agree.

00:24:57.100 --> 00:25:01.200
And I really like Joe's take on what this game could have been.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:08.860
But I almost read the whirlpools as something, like, that you wouldn't want to get...

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:09.880
Like, I didn't really...

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:13.440
Because it's a shmup, and you knew it was a shmup going in, you know that this works as,

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:17.840
like, a... You just gotta keep going forward until you get to the next stage kind of thing.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:21.080
And so, I didn't think I would want to get sucked up in a hole.

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:22.340
Right. Well, yeah.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.540
Well, because I thought that it was almost like a punishment.

00:25:24.700 --> 00:25:26.780
Like, you come across this little obstacle.

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.940
If it hits you, well, now you gotta do this little extra level in order to get back on

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:30.860
track. It fell in the hole. Yeah.

00:25:31.420 --> 00:25:35.680
And the minus stage is definitely designed in a way that's supposed to make you lose

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:40.520
your power-ups or even, you know, go as far as losing the lives.

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:44.520
And when you lose a life, it is pretty cool that it just automatically regenerates you.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:45.620
Yeah, I do appreciate that.

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:51.320
Appreciate that. And even the continuation system, too, which, as long as you have hearts collected, you can,

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:56.240
I think, you get up to nine, you're able to, say, continue and move on.

00:25:56.320 --> 00:26:00.860
Now, granted, there's only five stages in the game, so I don't think it needed that complicated

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:03.780
of a continuing life system.

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:05.420
But I'm all for it.

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:11.880
I really expected, the first time I died was on the horizontal stage, and I expected

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.560
to have to, like, redo that whole room, and I was kind of pissed.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:19.580
And then I just immediately came back out, and I was like, oh, it's kind of nice.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:21.740
It's appreciated. I don't think I remember that.

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:25.160
Yeah. Can't think of anything like that, actually.

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:26.420
.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:37.120
.

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:05.320
Capcom did make this game in 1986 for the arcade, even though it's now 1988.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:11.980
But it's around the same time as, say, Gradius was done by Konami.

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:16.380
So it's like they did have other shmups as a point of reference.

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:18.020
This was just their way of doing it.

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.600
Capcom maybe did 1942.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:24.460
I can't remember.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:27.960
I don't think so.

00:27:28.060 --> 00:27:30.840
I don't think they did. I think that might have been Irem now that I think about it.

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:33.740
Who did that? Nope, it was Capcom.

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:35.020
Very cool. Yep.

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:36.700
Very legal. It was Capcom.

00:27:36.760 --> 00:27:37.580
Very cool and very legal.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:43.960
And so Capcom has done shmups before and they just seem to not be stepping it up to Konami's level.

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:45.520
But at least it's an improvement.

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:46.520
But it is an improvement.

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:50.900
Absolutely. And the game was made or designed, rather, as a team of people.

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:57.620
But the designer was Akira Kitamura, who we talked about extensively on the Mega Man episode.

00:27:57.620 --> 00:28:01.220
He is the director and artist of Mega Man.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:16.760
Nice. I mean, like, it's just weird to think that Mega Man and Legendary Wings can happen around the same time and one game be so revolutionary and talk to so many people and the other one never mentioned again.

00:28:17.080 --> 00:28:19.220
Just forgotten. Yeah. But that's just how things are sometimes.

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:25.100
And for all we know, you know, who knows what the video game industry in Japan was like in the late 80s.

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:27.620
But it might have just been like, Kitamura, you're doing this.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:30.000
You know, like some guy programmed it.

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:31.060
You're the director.

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:32.760
Like, go direct.

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:36.720
Yeah. Go figure out, like, you know, the art style for the game and stuff like that.

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:38.840
Like, you know, oh, I think it should be fantasy themed.

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:39.940
And there's a dark supercomputer.

00:28:41.600 --> 00:28:45.000
Who knows? We don't have any interviews on Legendary Wings.

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:46.820
So that's that.

